replacements = {
    "5m_q16": """<div><div class="concept-heading">Naive Bayes Classifier</div><p>The Naive Bayes classifier is a probabilistic machine learning model based on Bayes' Theorem. It calculates the probability of different classes based on various attributes and selects the class with the highest probability.</p><p><b>Mathematical Foundation:</b></p><ul><li><b>Bayes' Theorem Equation:</b> $P(y|X) = \\frac{P(X|y) \\cdot P(y)}{P(X)}$</li><li><b>Posterior Probability $P(y|X)$:</b> The probability of a specific class $y$ being the correct output, given the observed feature set $X$.</li><li><b>Likelihood $P(X|y)$:</b> The probability of observing the specific feature set $X$, assuming that class $y$ is true.</li><li><b>Prior Probability $P(y)$:</b> The baseline probability of class $y$ occurring in the dataset, regardless of any features.</li><li><b>Evidence $P(X)$:</b> The total probability of the feature set $X$ occurring across all possible classes.</li></ul><p><b>Key Assumptions (The "Naive" Aspect):</b></p><ul><li><b>Conditional Independence:</b> The fundamental assumption is that all features in $X$ are conditionally independent of one another, given the class label $y$.</li><li><b>Feature Decomposition:</b> Because of this independence, the joint likelihood can be broken down into a product of individual feature probabilities: $P(X|y) = P(x_1|y) \\times P(x_2|y) \\times \\dots \\times P(x_n|y)$.</li><li><b>Practical Implication:</b> In real-world data, features are frequently correlated. Despite the assumption being theoretically inaccurate (or "naive"), the model often maintains high predictive accuracy in practice.</li></ul></div><p><strong>Comprehensive Analysis:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Scalability:</strong> Highly computationally efficient, making it ideal for extremely large datasets like text corpora.</li><li><strong>Robustness:</strong> Naturally handles high-dimensional data and requires a relatively small amount of training data to estimate probabilities.</li><li><strong>Interpretability:</strong> Provides clear probabilistic outputs that are easy to analyze mathematically.</li></ul><p><strong>Practical Code Snippet:</strong></p><pre><code>import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n\n# Example placeholder for concept implementation\ndef apply_concept(data):\n    print('Processing data based on ML principles...')\n    return data * 2\n</code></pre><div style="margin-top: 15px; padding: 15px; background: rgba(0, 200, 150, 0.1); border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-teal); border-radius: 8px;"><strong>💡 Tip to remember:</strong> "Naive" means assuming every feature votes for the final class entirely on its own, without consulting any other features. Bayes' theorem is just updating your initial guess (Prior) based on new evidence (Likelihood).</div>""",
    "5m_q17": """<div><div class="concept-heading">Support Vector Machines (SVM)</div><p>SVM is a supervised learning algorithm that determines the optimal decision boundary to separate classes in an $N$-dimensional space.</p><ul><li><b>Hyperplane:</b> This is the decision boundary that separates the classes. Its dimensionality depends on the features: it is a point in 1D, a line in 2D, and a flat plane in 3D.</li><li><b>Support Vectors:</b> These are the specific data points that lie closest to the hyperplane. They are critical because they define the margin; changing or removing them alters the position of the hyperplane.</li><li><b>Maximum Margin:</b> The objective of SVM is to identify the hyperplane that maximizes the margin. The margin is the orthogonal distance between the hyperplane and the closest support vectors from each class. A wider margin indicates better generalization to unseen data.</li></ul><p><b>Hard Margin vs. Soft Margin (Hinge Loss):</b></p><ul><li><b>Hard Margin:</b> Assumes the data is perfectly linearly separable. It strictly separates classes with zero misclassifications. It is highly sensitive to outliers and fails if classes overlap.</li><li><b>Soft Margin (Hinge Loss):</b> Used when data is not perfectly separable. It introduces a regularization parameter ($C$) and slack variables. This permits a controlled number of misclassifications, leading to a boundary that is more robust to noise and outliers.</li></ul></div><p><strong>Comprehensive Analysis:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Scalability:</strong> Training time can be high for very large datasets, but inference is fast as it only relies on support vectors.</li><li><strong>Robustness:</strong> Soft margin optimization makes the model highly resilient to outliers.</li><li><strong>Interpretability:</strong> The margin and support vectors provide clear geometric intuition for the model's decisions.</li></ul><p><strong>Practical Code Snippet:</strong></p><pre><code>import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n\n# Example placeholder for concept implementation\ndef apply_concept(data):\n    print('Processing data based on ML principles...')\n    return data * 2\n</code></pre><div class="diagram-container" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">\n<svg viewBox="0 0 300 200" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse">\n      <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n  <line x1="0" y1="100" x2="300" y2="100" stroke="#444" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="2"/>\n  <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="5" fill="#ff4d4d"/>\n  <circle cx="80" cy="70" r="5" fill="#ff4d4d"/>\n  <circle cx="100" cy="40" r="5" fill="#ff4d4d"/>\n  <circle cx="120" cy="80" r="5" fill="#ff4d4d" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/> \n  <circle cx="200" cy="150" r="5" fill="#4da6ff"/>\n  <circle cx="220" cy="120" r="5" fill="#4da6ff"/>\n  <circle cx="250" cy="170" r="5" fill="#4da6ff"/>\n  <circle cx="180" cy="110" r="5" fill="#4da6ff" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/> \n  <line x1="60" y1="180" x2="250" y2="20" stroke="var(--primary-teal)" stroke-width="3"/>\n  <text x="210" y="20" fill="var(--primary-teal)" font-weight="bold" font-size="10">Hyperplane</text>\n  <line x1="30" y1="160" x2="220" y2="0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4"/>\n  <line x1="90" y1="200" x2="280" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4"/>\n  <line x1="120" y1="80" x2="180" y2="110" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrow)" marker-start="url(#arrow)"/>\n  <text x="135" y="105" fill="currentColor" font-size="10" transform="rotate(27 150 95)">Margin</text>\n  <text x="30" y="20" fill="currentColor" font-size="12" font-weight="bold">SVM Decision Boundary</text>\n</svg>\n</div><div style="margin-top: 15px; padding: 15px; background: rgba(0, 200, 150, 0.1); border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-teal); border-radius: 8px;"><strong>💡 Tip to remember:</strong> Think of the Hyperplane as a road, and the Margin as the width of the road. Support Vectors are the houses right on the edge of the road. Hard Margin means no houses can be on the road, while Soft Margin allows a few mailboxes to slightly cross the line to keep the road mostly straight.</div>""",
    "5m_q18": """<div><div class="concept-heading">SVM Kernel Trick</div><p><b>The Kernel Trick:</b></p><ul><li>Standard SVM classifiers are designed to find linear boundaries (straight lines or planes).</li><li>When data cannot be separated linearly, the "Kernel Trick" is used. It applies a mathematical transformation to map the original data into a higher-dimensional space.</li><li>In this higher dimension, a linear hyperplane can successfully separate the classes.</li><li>The "trick" is that it calculates the relationships (dot products) between data points in this higher space efficiently, without ever explicitly calculating their new coordinates. This saves massive computational resources.</li></ul><p><b>Types of Kernels:</b></p><ul><li><b>Linear Kernel:</b> <ul><li>Computes standard dot products without complex transformations.</li><li><b>Usage:</b> Best when the data is already linearly separable in its original form. It is the fastest kernel to train.</li></ul></li><li><b>Polynomial Kernel:</b> <ul><li>Maps data into a polynomial feature space of a specified degree $d$.</li><li><b>Usage:</b> Effective for datasets where the decision boundary curves or forms specific polynomial shapes. Often applied in image processing tasks.</li></ul></li><li><b>Radial Basis Function (RBF) Kernel:</b> <ul><li>The most widely used and versatile kernel. It transforms data into an infinite-dimensional space using exponential functions.</li><li>It measures similarity based on the distance between points, controlled by a parameter gamma ($\\gamma$).</li><li><b>Usage:</b> Excels at creating highly localized, complex boundaries (like islands) around clusters of data points.</li></ul></li></ul></div><p><strong>Comprehensive Analysis:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Scalability:</strong> Using kernels (especially RBF) can be computationally expensive on very large datasets due to the calculation of distance between every pair of points.</li><li><strong>Robustness:</strong> Kernels allow SVM to handle highly complex, non-linear relationships in the data without requiring manual feature engineering.</li><li><strong>Interpretability:</strong> While linear kernels are easy to interpret, RBF and Polynomial kernels act as "black boxes," making it difficult to understand the exact decision boundary in the original space.</li></ul><p><strong>Practical Code Snippet:</strong></p><pre><code>import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n\n# Example placeholder for concept implementation\ndef apply_concept(data):\n    print('Processing data based on ML principles...')\n    return data * 2\n</code></pre><div style="margin-top: 15px; padding: 15px; background: rgba(0, 200, 150, 0.1); border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-teal); border-radius: 8px;"><strong>💡 Tip to remember:</strong> Imagine red and blue balls mixed on a flat table (2D) that you can't separate with a straight stick. The Kernel Trick is like throwing the balls into the air (3D); while they are at different heights, you slide a flat sheet of cardboard between the red and blue balls.</div>""",
    "5m_q19": """<div><div class="concept-heading">Bagging vs. Boosting Ensemble Methods</div><p>Ensemble methods are techniques that combine multiple "weak" machine learning models (often simple decision trees) to produce a single, highly accurate "strong" model.</p><p><b>Bagging (Bootstrap Aggregating) & Random Forest:</b></p><ul><li><b>Training Process:</b> Multiple models are trained <b>independently and in parallel</b>.</li><li><b>Data Sampling:</b> Each model is trained on a random subset of the original training data, drawn with replacement (Bootstrap sampling).</li><li><b>Aggregation:</b> The final prediction is determined by combining the outputs of all models. This is done via majority voting for classification tasks, or averaging for regression tasks.</li><li><b>Random Forest:</b> A specific type of Bagging that uses Decision Trees. It adds extra randomness by selecting only a random subset of features at each split in the tree.</li><li><b>Primary Benefit:</b> Bagging dramatically reduces <b>variance</b>, making the model less prone to overfitting on noisy data.</li></ul><p><b>Boosting (e.g., XGBoost, AdaBoost):</b></p><ul><li><b>Training Process:</b> Multiple models are trained <b>sequentially</b> (one after the other).</li><li><b>Error Correction:</b> Each new model is specifically designed to focus on correcting the errors (misclassified instances or residuals) made by the sequence of previous models.</li><li><b>AdaBoost:</b> Adjusts the weights of the training data points. Data points that were classified incorrectly get higher weights, forcing the next model to focus on them.</li><li><b>Gradient Boosting (XGBoost):</b> Instead of adjusting weights, subsequent trees are trained directly to predict the residual errors left by the previous trees.</li><li><b>Primary Benefit:</b> Boosting primarily reduces <b>bias</b> (underfitting), resulting in highly accurate models. However, it can be more prone to overfitting than Bagging if not properly tuned.</li></ul></div><p><strong>Comprehensive Analysis:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Scalability:</strong> Bagging models can be easily parallelized, making them highly scalable. Boosting models are sequential, making training slower, though modern implementations like XGBoost are highly optimized.</li><li><strong>Robustness:</strong> Bagging is highly robust to noisy data and outliers. Boosting is sensitive to outliers because it heavily prioritizes fixing difficult-to-predict points.</li><li><strong>Interpretability:</strong> Ensembles are generally harder to interpret than single decision trees, but feature importance scores can still be extracted.</li></ul><p><strong>Practical Code Snippet:</strong></p><pre><code>import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n\n# Example placeholder for concept implementation\ndef apply_concept(data):\n    print('Processing data based on ML principles...')\n    return data * 2\n</code></pre><div style="margin-top: 15px; padding: 15px; background: rgba(0, 200, 150, 0.1); border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-teal); border-radius: 8px;"><strong>💡 Tip to remember:</strong> Bagging is like asking a group of experts for their opinions independently and taking a vote (Random Forest). Boosting is like a relay race where each runner tries to make up for the specific mistakes of the previous runner (XGBoost).</div>""",
    "5m_q20": """<div><div class="concept-heading">Confusion Matrix and Metrics</div><p><b>Confusion Matrix:</b> A standard $2 \\times 2$ table used to evaluate the performance of a binary classification model by comparing predicted classes to actual classes.</p><ul><li><b>True Positive (TP):</b> The model correctly predicted the Positive class. (Actual is Positive, Predicted is Positive).</li><li><b>True Negative (TN):</b> The model correctly predicted the Negative class. (Actual is Negative, Predicted is Negative).</li><li><b>False Positive (FP):</b> The model incorrectly predicted the Positive class. Also known as a <b>Type I Error</b>. (Actual is Negative, Predicted is Positive).</li><li><b>False Negative (FN):</b> The model incorrectly predicted the Negative class. Also known as a <b>Type II Error</b>. (Actual is Positive, Predicted is Negative).</li></ul><p><b>Evaluation Formulas:</b></p><ul><li><b>Accuracy:</b> $\\frac{TP + TN}{TP + TN + FP + FN}$. Measures the overall percentage of correct predictions across all classes.</li><li><b>Precision:</b> $\\frac{TP}{TP + FP}$. Measures exactness: Out of all the instances the model predicted as positive, how many were actually positive?</li><li><b>Recall (Sensitivity):</b> $\\frac{TP}{TP + FN}$. Measures completeness: Out of all the actual positive instances in the data, how many did the model successfully find?</li><li><b>F1-Score:</b> $2 \\times \\frac{Precision \\times Recall}{Precision + Recall}$. This is the Harmonic Mean of Precision and Recall.</li></ul><p><b>Importance of F1-Score for Imbalanced Data:</b></p><ul><li>Consider a dataset where 99% of transactions are legitimate (Negative) and 1% are fraud (Positive).</li><li>A naive model that predicts "Legitimate" for every transaction will achieve 99% <b>Accuracy</b>, but it is entirely useless for catching fraud.</li><li>The <b>F1-Score</b> resolves this because its formula completely ignores True Negatives (TN). It forces the evaluation to focus strictly on how well the model identifies the rare positive cases (Recall) without triggering excessive false alarms (Precision).</li></ul></div><p><strong>Comprehensive Analysis:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Scalability:</strong> Computing these metrics is extremely fast and scalable, requiring only simple arithmetic on the final predictions.</li><li><strong>Robustness:</strong> Relying on F1-Score rather than Accuracy provides a much more robust evaluation for real-world datasets, which are rarely perfectly balanced.</li><li><strong>Interpretability:</strong> These metrics provide a clear numerical summary that helps stakeholders balance the trade-offs between missing positive cases (FN) and generating false alarms (FP).</li></ul><p><strong>Practical Code Snippet:</strong></p><pre><code>import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n\n# Example placeholder for concept implementation\ndef apply_concept(data):\n    print('Processing data based on ML principles...')\n    return data * 2\n</code></pre><div style="margin-top: 15px; padding: 15px; background: rgba(0, 200, 150, 0.1); border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-teal); border-radius: 8px;"><strong>💡 Tip to remember:</strong> Precision is "Quality" (Are the alarms real?). Recall is "Quantity" (Did we catch all the bad guys?). F1-Score is the balanced average that punishes extreme imbalances between the two.</div>""",
    "num_q1": """<div><div class="concept-heading">Linear Regression Problem</div><p><b>Objective:</b> Find the simple linear regression equation $y = b_0 + b_1x$ using the least-squares method, and predict the marks for 6 study hours.</p><p><b>Step 1: Construct the Calculation Table</b></p>\n<table style="border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 15px;">\n<tr style="border-bottom: 2px solid #ccc;"><th>Study Hours ($X$)</th><th>Exam Marks ($Y$)</th><th>$X \\times Y$</th><th>$X^2$</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>35</td><td>35</td><td>1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>40</td><td>80</td><td>4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>50</td><td>150</td><td>9</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>55</td><td>220</td><td>16</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>65</td><td>325</td><td>25</td></tr>\n<tr style="border-top: 2px solid #ccc; font-weight: bold;"><td>$\\Sigma X = 15$</td><td>$\\Sigma Y = 245$</td><td>$\\Sigma XY = 810$</td><td>$\\Sigma X^2 = 55$</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><b>Step 2: Calculate the Means</b></p><ul><li>Number of observations ($n$) = 5</li><li>Mean of $X$ ($\\bar{x}$) = $\\frac{\\Sigma X}{n} = \\frac{15}{5} = 3$</li><li>Mean of $Y$ ($\\bar{y}$) = $\\frac{\\Sigma Y}{n} = \\frac{245}{5} = 49$</li></ul><p><b>Step 3: Calculate the Slope ($b_1$)</b></p><ul><li>Formula: $b_1 = \\frac{\\Sigma XY - n \\cdot \\bar{x} \\cdot \\bar{y}}{\\Sigma X^2 - n \\cdot \\bar{x}^2}$</li><li>Substitute values: $b_1 = \\frac{810 - (5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 49)}{55 - (5 \\cdot 3^2)}$</li><li>Calculate numerator: $810 - 735 = 75$</li><li>Calculate denominator: $55 - 45 = 10$</li><li>Result: $b_1 = \\frac{75}{10} = 7.5$</li></ul><p><b>Step 4: Calculate the Intercept ($b_0$)</b></p><ul><li>Formula: $b_0 = \\bar{y} - (b_1 \\cdot \\bar{x})$</li><li>Substitute values: $b_0 = 49 - (7.5 \\cdot 3)$</li><li>Calculate: $b_0 = 49 - 22.5 = 26.5$</li></ul><p><b>Step 5: Formulate Equation and Predict</b></p><ul><li><b>Final Regression Equation:</b> $y = 26.5 + 7.5x$</li><li><b>Prediction for $x = 6$:</b> $y = 26.5 + 7.5(6) = 26.5 + 45 = \\mathbf{71.5}$ marks</li></ul></div><p><strong>Comprehensive Analysis:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Scalability:</strong> The least-squares method requires a single pass over the data to compute the sums, making it highly efficient.</li><li><strong>Robustness:</strong> Ordinary least squares is highly sensitive to outliers, which can heavily skew the calculated slope.</li><li><strong>Interpretability:</strong> The resulting equation provides direct interpretability: for every extra hour studied ($x$), the exam score ($y$) is predicted to increase by $7.5$ points.</li></ul><p><strong>Practical Code Snippet:</strong></p><pre><code>import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n\n# Example placeholder for concept implementation\ndef apply_concept(data):\n    print('Processing data based on ML principles...')\n    return data * 2\n</code></pre><div style="margin-top: 15px; padding: 15px; background: rgba(0, 200, 150, 0.1); border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-teal); border-radius: 8px;"><strong>💡 Tip to remember:</strong> The slope $b_1$ formula is just the covariance of X and Y divided by the variance of X. Always calculate your means ($\\bar{x}, \\bar{y}$) first to simplify the process.</div>""",
    "num_q2": """<div><div class="concept-heading">Linear Regression Calculation</div><p><b>Objective:</b> Calculate the values of $b_0$ and $b_1$ and formulate the final regression equation $y = b_0 + b_1x$ given the summary statistics.</p><p><b>Step 1: Review the Given Data</b></p><ul><li>Number of samples ($n$) = 5</li><li>Sum of $x$ ($\\Sigma x$) = 20</li><li>Sum of $y$ ($\\Sigma y$) = 75</li><li>Sum of $x^2$ ($\\Sigma x^2$) = 90</li><li>Sum of $xy$ ($\\Sigma xy$) = 340</li></ul><p><b>Step 2: Calculate the Means</b></p><ul><li>Mean of $x$ ($\\bar{x}$) = $\\frac{\\Sigma x}{n} = \\frac{20}{5} = 4$</li><li>Mean of $y$ ($\\bar{y}$) = $\\frac{\\Sigma y}{n} = \\frac{75}{5} = 15$</li></ul><p><b>Step 3: Calculate the Slope ($b_1$)</b></p><ul><li>Formula: $b_1 = \\frac{\\Sigma xy - n \\cdot \\bar{x} \\cdot \\bar{y}}{\\Sigma x^2 - n \\cdot \\bar{x}^2}$</li><li>Substitute values: $b_1 = \\frac{340 - (5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 15)}{90 - (5 \\cdot 4^2)}$</li><li>Calculate numerator: $340 - 300 = 40$</li><li>Calculate denominator: $90 - (5 \\cdot 16) = 90 - 80 = 10$</li><li>Result: $b_1 = \\frac{40}{10} = 4$</li></ul><p><b>Step 4: Calculate the Intercept ($b_0$)</b></p><ul><li>Formula: $b_0 = \\bar{y} - (b_1 \\cdot \\bar{x})$</li><li>Substitute values: $b_0 = 15 - (4 \\cdot 4)$</li><li>Calculate: $b_0 = 15 - 16 = -1$</li></ul><p><b>Step 5: Formulate the Final Equation</b></p><ul><li><b>Final Regression Equation:</b> $y = -1 + 4x$</li></ul></div><p><strong>Comprehensive Analysis:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Scalability:</strong> Using pre-computed summary statistics is exceptionally fast, demonstrating how large datasets can be summarized into minimal statistics.</li><li><strong>Robustness:</strong> This deterministic mathematical formula will always yield the precise optimal line that minimizes squared errors for the given data.</li><li><strong>Interpretability:</strong> The intercept $b_0 = -1$ mathematically indicates the predicted $y$ value when $x=0$, even if a negative value might lack practical meaning depending on the real-world context of the data.</li></ul><p><strong>Practical Code Snippet:</strong></p><pre><code>import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n\n# Example placeholder for concept implementation\ndef apply_concept(data):\n    print('Processing data based on ML principles...')\n    return data * 2\n</code></pre><div style="margin-top: 15px; padding: 15px; background: rgba(0, 200, 150, 0.1); border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-teal); border-radius: 8px;"><strong>💡 Tip to remember:</strong> The order of operations matters. Always calculate the slope ($b_1$) first because the intercept ($b_0$) requires the slope to be computed.</div>"""
}

